\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let me read the Hebrew text of Ezekiel 30 more carefully first.\
\
\
<notes> Key observations on first reading of Ezekiel 30:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The first oracle (vv. 1-19) lacks a date formula - unusual. It opens with 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  language that echoes broader prophetic "day of YHWH" tradition (Joel, Amos, Zephaniah). Verse 3: 
\f2 \uc0\u1511 \u1464 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1489 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1511 \u1464 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1489 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1497 \u1492 \u1465 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  - the repetition of 
\f2 \uc0\u1511 \u1464 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1489 
\f1  is striking.\
The second oracle (vv. 20-26) IS dated: year 11, first month, seventh day. This places it about 3 months before Jerusalem's fall.\
What strikes me most in the first oracle is the shift from the cosmic/universal language of vv. 2-3 to the extremely specific geographic catalog in vv. 13-18. The rhetoric moves from "day of YHWH" and "time of nations" to naming individual Egyptian cities one by one.\
The arms oracle (vv. 20-26) uses 
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  - "MY sword" - given to Babylon's hand. This is the wages/employer relationship from 29:17-21 made concrete.\
Connection to chapter 29: the previous instance noted that 29's key was 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 . Here in 30:22 we get 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1508 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1506 \u1465 \u1492 
\f1  - same construction. And v. 12's 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1512 \u1464 \u1489 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  directly answers Pharaoh's claim "the Nile is mine." </notes>\
What strikes me first about the rhetoric of this first oracle isn't actually the "day of YHWH" language, though that's important. It's the movement from cosmic to cartographic.\
The oracle opens with the most universal language Ezekiel has used in the Egypt oracles: 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1504 \u1464 \u1503 
\f1  
