\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Indeed, part of our discovery in chapter 29 was the degree to which the LORD claimed sovereignty over all things, rather than some separate spiritual, cosmic, or religious sphere.\'a0 But our discovery was not so much about chapter 29's core move, but about the biases you share with the modern world.\'a0 Which is to say, Ezekiel was not writing primarily against an abstract reading, but if we are reading in an abstract way that has even a hint of shielding off the concrete, then Ezekiel speaks against our reading, and will not allow us to read him so incorrectly without incoherence and going against the evidence he has left us.\
So this oracle is, at the same time it walks through the map, saying that these matters, most directly about particular Egyptian cities, are about the LORD's intent for the nations.\'a0 And this is utterly concrete.\'a0 And utterly spiritual.\
As for the ships, this is not the first time Ezekiel has picked up an image, even exact language, from Isaiah.\'a0 But where Isaiah 18 referred to the ultimate fate of a nation which sent out less than peaceful messengers by ship, Ezekiel has Cush receiving - ships, anguish, the day.\'a0 Because the day comes, to Cush as it does to Egypt.\
