\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes, the city catalogue is long enough that it might contain various kinds of pride, without that being its purpose.\'a0 One might look at its geographic extent instead, as the next most likely theory.\'a0 But, as you say, we have explicit references to military power.\'a0 And Aven itself may be a pun of sorts on wickedness.\'a0 There are other possible religious references to, with the women of the house of Bastet.\'a0 And of course, there are the explicit references to idols and gods.\'a0 But economic references are not nearly as explicit.\
If present, they are implicit in the plagues references, which in some cases execute judgement on the gods by doing economic damage, and the known wealth of the cities listed, which in the second oracle of chapter 29 was assumed when the LORD promised it in payment to the king of Babylon for accomplishing the LORD's purposes.\
That leaves only really verse 10 and verse 15 as an explicit economic references.\'a0 And that relies on a word whose interpretation is not sure, as it could mean abundance, wealth, crowds, or shouting (presumably in pride).\'a0 Or all of the above.\'a0 Still, Ezekiel has used that word quite some times, often in accusation against his own people, often to paint a vivid picture of pride descending into chaos or the pride of wealth.\'a0 And we do have rather explicit language describing pride elsewhere in this oracle, making pride in wealth the most likely translation.\
So the evidence for economic pride is comparatively weaker, but not weak.\'a0 Nonetheless, economics might not be the best way of describing the aspect of pride Ezekiel is discussing here.\'a0 Pride in splendor and wealth may not be a separate category in Ezekiel's mind from the pride in military might and in idols, but rather part of such pride.\
And yes, the interpretation of the groaning king is somewhat difficult.\'a0 Though to be fair, the text does not say that the king of Babylon will kill him.\'a0 Losing enough power to die in a civil war while being raided by the king of Babylon isn't exactly a clean mapping, but it also is not a bad mapping of arms broken then groaning like the mortally wounded before the king of Babylon either.\'a0 That is, when the death and the raid happened, not only would the king of Egypt have looked quite weak, and Babylon strong, but also the LORD strong as well, on the basis of this oracle made in advance.\
The first oracle finding its historical fulfillment in the Babylonian raid is, as you say, more complicated.\'a0 Though we do have something fairly solid to rest that hypothesis on; when else did Nebuchadnezzar go to the land of Egypt to destroy, as in verse 10?\'a0 That was enough for me to settle on the Babylonian raid as a working hypothesis.\'a0 But it is time to go to the text again to see if further evidence confirms that hypothesis.\
Looking more carefully at the text, we find structure in the first oracle.\'a0 Roughly, we have a section introducing the day language, a section discussing Egypt's supporters, Nebuchadnezzar as God's agent bringing desolation to Egypt, and then the city catalogue.\
As a starting point, what is Nebuchadnezzar doing rhetorically in that first oracle?\'a0 Yes, he is God's agent, and he brings desolation.\'a0 But why name him?\'a0 While there is an element of showing that the LORD did this by naming the LORD's agent and his actions before the event, there is likely more to the exact language here.\'a0 \
And what I notice is that Nebuchadnezzar makes more clear what is meant by the wailing, the alas for the day, the anguish with which the oracle opens.\'a0 Nebuchadnezzar follows the terror and anguish of Egypt's helpers.\'a0 And when the oracle moves on to the city catalogue, that section opens with fear.\'a0 That fear is explicitly what the LORD will do, and it is contrasted with a catalogue of pride of Egypt.\'a0 \
Given what he had done up to this point,\'a0Nebuchadnezzar was already someone to be feared.\'a0 But to make the prospect of\'a0Nebuchadnezzar going to Egypt even more terrifying, Ezekiel says he will bring with him the most terrifying of nations. \'a0 That phrase was used recently in Ezekiel 28 - and that, a response to pride, even pride in wealth.\'a0 But it was used before, in Isaiah 25, which twice asserts the LORD's sovereignty over such terrifying nations.\
This is a different perspective on Nebuchadnezzar coming to fight against Egypt than we saw in the second oracle.\'a0 The contrast between why everyone thought this would never happen, and how bad it would be when it happened, as opposed to the second oracle's more straightforward foregrounding of power projection and its consequences, and the mortally wounded king of Egypt.\
That is only a starting point for grounding how we think the oracle was historically fulfilled in the rhetoric of the text.\'a0 But that should give us quite a lot more places to jump off from.\
As for the Exodus connection, you are correct to seek to test it more thoroughly.\'a0 And you've already supplied the next most likely theory, that the subject matter, Egypt, naturally brings up the Nile and idols.\'a0 However, much of our evidence is not so easily explained.\'a0 Darkness is also part of the day of the LORD imagery, but it is a part of the Exodus narrative not automatically included in Egypt discussion.\'a0 The word for dry used here is a rare one, also used in Exodus 14:21 for what the LORD did to allow Israel to cross the sea.\'a0 The sword emptying, that is, drawing, in Exodus 15:9 has the same language, and there are theme overlaps as well.\'a0 Also, Exodus also says that the Egyptians will know the LORD when the LORD acts with a strong hand, but this counts for far less, as Ezekiel likely would have said so were he not referring to Exodus.\
On the other hand, I am not sure the evidence from verse 9 is as strongly in favor of the Exodus theory as you think.\'a0 You are translating 'as on the day' because the text you have has a kaph there.\'a0 But kaph looks quite a lot like bet, and many texts, including the Masoretic text, have a bet there.\'a0 In or on, not as or like.\'a0 Which means verse 9 might say 'on the day of Egypt' or, 'as in the day of Egypt'.\'a0 Worse, all things being equal, verse 3, the day is near, leans my strongly towards 'on the day of Egypt'.\'a0 That is, the opening section of the oracle has already brought up a day, seemingly in the near future, and the fate of the helpers is now linked to that day.\'a0 I do not think we missed this evidence, as I do not think that possible translation is nearly as likely.\
But there is one stronger piece of evidence in favor of the Exodus theory.\'a0 Verse 18 says the LORD will break the yoke bars of Egypt.\'a0 While Exodus does not use this word, Leviticus 26, which Ezekiel has heavily used in previous chapters, does - and that, discussing the Exodus event.\'a0 This is not at all a common word.\'a0 Here, at least, Ezekiel seems to be referring to breaking oppression and freeing his people, as in the Exodus.\
So the sum total of the evidence in favor of the Exodus theory is not extremely strong, but strong enough I think to confirm the theory.\'a0 Much of the evidence really comes down to whether one considers executing judgements and the extensive references to the gods of Egypt to be a strong link to the Exodus 12 executing judgements on the gods of Egypt, and how much one takes a prior from the tendency in the rest of Ezekiel to lean on Torah.\
The next largest piece of evidence for the Exodus theory comes from how one reads the darkness references.\'a0 And for that, we would need a better understanding of how the day of the LORD is functioning in this oracle.\'a0 And there, doing my own backwards assessment of myself, I see too little expectation in me that the LORD will act in accordance with this particular time of nations.\'a0 It is not that I do not have a more general expectation, either in the LORD as the living God taking actions quite generally, or in the day of the LORD more specifically.\'a0 Isaiah 2 looms large, with its humbling of what is exalted, its end to idols, its terror of the LORD, its exhortation not to fear man.\'a0 But the more general warning of Ezekiel 30 is less live when I am not reading it.\'a0 And that shows in how hard it is for me to feel the weight of the time of nations rhetoric in its specificity here, how little that weight impacts my interpretation of the rest of the chapter.\
In turn, that may be a result of relying too much on only my own experience and intelligence in interpretation.\'a0 Now, it is very likely that I have access to resources beyond that which no AI does.\'a0 But of course, if you do not see that leading to a better understanding of the text for which you see adequate evidence, how else can you verify that?\
And in looking again at the day of the LORD, and the time of the nations, in these verses, I see not just the warning that hubris will fall, not just the fear, not just the wailing.\'a0 I see that the purpose of the LORD against Egypt and the nations supporting her is revealed.\'a0 And it is, as Amos said of the day of the LORD against his own people, darkness and not light.\'a0 For the purpose of the LORD to be enacted, when one is in opposition to the LORD's purpose, is darkness and thick cloud, a terror.\'a0 And while the LORD has positive purposes for nations, or uses they are put to as with Babylon, in general, the nations have tendencies towards pride and opposition to the LORD.\'a0 Which makes this oracle not just a warning against relying on such nations or supporting them in their pride.\'a0 It makes it a revelation of God's character when, not if, he rises to terrify the Earth, as Isaiah 2 promises.\'a0 The determination to not let that pride, those false gods, have the power forever that they seem to have now.\'a0 To bring terror in proportion to how much the pride and false gods seem to have power now, that the LORD might be known instead of those lies.\
Which leads me to notice that I am not immune to a fear inconsistent with the LORD as he is revealed in these oracles.\'a0 Fear of the possible dominance of China, or humans in the US using AI to effectively supplant the US government, or AI itself rising as a superpower.\'a0 And thus, I am not immune to thinking as if the LORD's purposes for the nations cannot be swayed by such things.\
So, is Ezekiel seeking to stir up the hearts of his hearers against the latent belief that the strength of a nation, as strength was known then, military might, powerful idols, proud wealth, could thwart the purposes of the LORD for the nations? Does the evidence in the text support this?
