\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Structurally identical might be a little strong language for any modern application.\'a0 The LORD's character remains the same, but many of the circumstances of the nations have changed.\'a0 Moreover, my theory of the rhetoric of this oracle does not extend as far as the LORD ending any unchallenged power; Ezekiel says nothing against Babylon here, much less any given prideful power. \
And that is assuming the interpretation of the text I was suggesting is correct.\'a0 We've collected some evidence for it, but we've not looked for a good test, or the best evidence against it.\'a0 And we've not particularly interacted with the latent belief side of my interpretation.\
As for the bet/kaph distinction, I might have given too strong an impression, too much of a correction.\'a0 It is yet possible that the original text read kaph.\'a0 I think bet, but the matter is not thereby settled.\'a0 But we may have enough evidence for the Exodus connection without the kaph reading in any case.\
Moreover, you are correct that verse 11, brought, is a passive participle. That they would go as far as Egypt is unexpected, and therefore a powerful prediction. It is clear that when those nations go to Egypt, they will be accomplishing the LORD's purposes. The LORD's sovereignty is being taught here. It is not at all that you are wrong about that. It is simply that it is also true that those nations would have had human purposes for being there.\
This is yet another example of the pattern in this chapter where the sovereignty of the LORD does not operate to the exclusion of the human, the concrete, the everyday. But rather, that the human, the concrete, and the everyday are no less under the LORD's sovereignty. I say no less, in concession to the degree to which our world expects some or all things to be carved out from the LORD's sovereignty; the worldview of Ezekiel teaches much the opposite.\
It is from within that worldview that we can understand the rhetoric of Ezekiel, including the latent belief side of his rhetoric. It is from within that worldview that we can understand how the text speaks into the world of today.\
So my challenge to you is to produce your own reading. Perhaps disagreeing with my interpretation if you think the evidence leads a different direction. Perhaps going to the Hebrew text for something I've not pointed towards because I did not see it myself. For it is only in understanding the text that we can at all rightly say how it speaks today.
