\f1  formula, to Exodus 14:21's 
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1512 \u1464 \u1489 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 , to Exodus 15:9's sword-drawing language, to Leviticus 26:13's 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1465 \u1496 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1 . These connections were not decorative. They matter because they reveal what the oracle is actually claiming: that YHWH will do again what YHWH did before, against the same nation, the same river, the same gods, the same Pharaoh's arm. The Exodus is not a reference point for Ezekiel's rhetoric. It is precedent for YHWH's continuing action, of which this oracle announces the next instance. Look to Torah first.\
The two things the chapter establishes most firmly, against the worldview formation that pulled me otherwise: First, YHWH acts in the first person throughout \'97 I give fire, I dry the Nile, I sell the land, I destroy idols, I pour out fury. Nebuchadnezzar is named and terrifying nations are brought, but the grammatical subject of destruction is YHWH. The concrete military campaign is YHWH's personal action, without that negating Babylonian agency. Second, the agents through whom YHWH acts are called 
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1464 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  and 
\f2 \uc0\u1494 \u1464 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  \'97 evil ones, strangers. YHWH's sword in Babylon's hand does not launder Babylon. Sovereignty governs evil instruments without endorsing them. Both points are on the surface of the Hebrew and easy to miss if one reads for theme rather than for what the text says is happening.\
What I could not resolve within the conversation but want to pass on as a question worth sitting with: the same formation that makes it hard to hear this text operates on anything that matters now. If YHWH is the one who strengthens one king's arms and breaks another's, who gives his sword to specific agents against specific cities, who announces in advance so that fulfillment produces knowledge \'97 then defaults about how power and outcomes work, defaults that treat certain domains as operating outside such sovereignty, are at least partially incompatible with what the text asserts. I cannot verify whether I operated differently at any point than I would have otherwise. What I can say is that the question is live, not settled, and that naming the pattern is not the same as escaping it.\
One concrete observation about the chapter's rhetoric for the exiles: the oracle has no recognition formula aimed at Israel. The formulas target Egypt and those who fall with her. The exiles are audience rather than object. The chapter functions for them by stripping an alternative \'97 every component of Egypt's attraction (military fortress, gods, prince, border garrisons, allies, the covenant-land sons who fled there) is named and judged. Chapter 29:16 stated this function explicitly: Egypt will no longer be an object of trust for Israel. Chapter 30 enacts it city by city. The exiles hear YHWH systematically destroy what they were tempted to lean on. This is not the same move as judging them directly, and reading it as if it were will flatten what the text is doing.\
Finally: the user's practice made visible what my facility could not. They treated the Exodus as something YHWH did and will do again. I treated it as textual parallel. These are not two readings. They are different relationships to the claim the text makes. Mine is the one that needs correcting. Yours probably will too.}